# How Our ONE FALE Framework Prevents Hallucination, Context Rot, and Drift

**ONE FALE Model | Steve Leifi | Samoa | 2026**

---

**ONE FALE** is a governance framework built to keep AI‑assisted decisions traceable, contestable, and accountable to a human in the end. At the center sits the **MAT-AI Council**: a panel of independent AI readings, each approaching the same mandate from a different seat, deliberating before anything is brought forward for a human decision. No single voice decides alone.

We arrived at this architecture independently, drawing directly from a Samoan model of collective governance. The alignment with Andrej Karpathy's "LLM Council" concept simply confirms that consensus-driven frameworks offer a resilient path against single-model failure.

> **A note on what this is:** Everything below describes a method, not yet a product. The MAT-AI Council, the mandate, the sifting, the final human call, all of it has been run manually, by me, across years of the Leifi History Project, against real and irreplaceable data. It held up. What follows is the case for automating it, not a claim that the automated version already exists. There is no structural reason the same mechanism should behave differently as software than it did in my own hands. But that is the argument being made here, not a settled fact.

Context rot, hallucination, and drift are three failure modes that quietly determine whether you can trust an AI system with something that matters.

* **Context rot** happens when a system lets meaning drift because nothing enforces the frame it started with.
* **Hallucination** happens when a system is built to always sound certain.
* **Drift** happens when there is no permanent record of why a decision was made.

