## Where This Came From

The framework did not emerge from theory. It came from necessity.

I was sitting in Lalomanu—the cool early mornings were the best times, the quiet before the world breaks into sunrise—reconstructing our family history across two Samoas. The Leifi History Project had grown beyond anything I anticipated: genealogical records, court findings, church registers, oral histories from Aleipata to Tutuila to Manu'a and back again.

For the most part I worked alone. I live out on the remotest south‑eastern coast of Upolu Island, in Lalomanu Village. But I had an internet connection, and I realised that AI could help me sift through all the information I had collected over 20 years. It was a practical choice. It saved me time and money. Right out of the gate, I purchased a cheap scanner/printer and set to work digitizing my family's history.

I started with Gemini, feeding it everything I had. The Gemini chat box began to fail, to act up; I noticed the pattern. The longer a conversation ran, the worse it got. So I tried a new chat, starting fresh with the core truths. That worked for a while, until it failed again.

Then I realised I could run different LLMs at the same time. Gemini in one window, Copilot in another, DeepSeek in a third. I added Claude, and Perplexity for good measure. Each processing the same material through their own lens. I would move the key truths from one to the next, using them as a handover rather than a restart.

It was clumsy. But it worked.

