## From Methodology to Architecture

Before there was an architecture, there was a method.

I came up with a method, a framework I called **OHMS (One History, Many Stories)** *(see article featured at present)*. It was built to hold diverse historical streams, oral traditions, archival documents, and foundational family accounts, without requiring them to compromise their distinct integrity.

The method was simple:
1. Gather every account without imposing a hierarchy.
2. Look for structural alignment.
3. Where multiple independent paths pointed toward the same conclusion, that convergence offered a reliable foundation—what we came to call a **Pillar of Certainty**.

We were discovering convergence. The *Leone Rosetta Stone*, a chapter in the Leifi History Project, became one of the clearest examples of this in practice. Two branches of a family, separated for more than a century, had each preserved their own records in isolation. When they were brought together, the names, marriages, and foundational ancestral structures aligned. Neither branch had coordinated with the other. The convergence was discovered, not engineered.

By then we were already using OHMS. The Leone discovery confirmed what the method had already shown us was possible. That confirmation became part of the foundation for the architecture that followed.

